a crime scene that has become all too  familiar for journalists across mexico  lordes maldonado lopez had been targeted  before for doing her job but this time  she didn't survive  gunmen shot her as she waited in a car  in tijuana's santa fe neighborhood  police received a call but it was too  late when they arrived at the scene  the secretary of security and citizen  protection had the responsibility to  continue to offer security to lourdes  maldonado she'd said that when she'd  arrive home in the evenings it was when  she felt most vulnerable she did not  have that protection  lopez is the second journalist to be  killed in mexico in less than a week and  the third this month alone on monday  another journalist margarito martinez  who covered police and crime stories was  also killed  the committee to protect journalists has  called on mexico to investigate the  attack thoroughly and transparently  the brutal reality of mexico nowadays is  that the federal state and the municipal  government simply don't investigate the  crimes against the population in general  or against human rights activists and  journalists  in 2019 lopez told mexican president  andres manuel lopez obrador she'd feared  for her life and asked for support it  followed a labor dispute where she sued  an influential former employee for  unfair dismissal a lawsuit she won a few  days ago  it is very painful what happened in  tijuana we will soon investigate she was  indeed here in the beginning of 2019 to  make a complaint about the labor issue  and she was assisted unfortunately this  happened we need to see if this  assassination is connected to the labor  issue or not and find who is responsible  mexico is one of the most dangerous  countries for journalists more than 100  media workers have been murdered in the  last couple of decades but only a  fraction of those crimes have led to  convictions  al jazeera
 

